---
/**
 * website/src/pages/privacy.astro — the privacy statement (website plan, Task W1.12;
 * reconciliation findings 16 and 18; decisions D93 and D106).
 *
 * The page a reader is owed once the site measures anything: what is collected, by what, when
 * it loads, how the answer is given and how it is taken back. It is also the page where the
 * choice is always available again — `Base.astro` is asked for `alwaysOffer` here, so the
 * banner appears on this page even for a visitor who already decided, which is what makes
 * consent withdrawable from the one page that says how (W1.12 step 3).
 *
 * **Every sentence about measurement is a fact the build holds, and nothing is invented.**
 * The page reads `site.yaml` through the content pipeline, exactly as the layout does, and
 * branches on `analytics.enabled`: with the flag off — the state the site ships in today —
 * it says the site collects nothing, and it names no vendor, because there is no vendor to
 * name. With the flag on it states what the site's own loader does, which is one page view
 * and one setting (`anonymize_ip`), and it does not restate Google's policy for Google. Two
 * of its claims are held to the artifact by `website/test/consent.mjs`: that no page carries
 * a form or a field, and that the tool it names is the tool the build would load.
 *
 * The page carries no script of its own and no second copy of the consent key: the banner is
 * the layout's, rendered here because this page asks for it. The styles are scoped, token-only
 * (no colour literal, so the palette stays W1.2's) and add no behaviour.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadSite } from '../content.ts'

// The facts, through the pipeline the layout uses: a site.yaml this page cannot read stops
// the build rather than shipping a statement about a configuration nobody checked (W1.3).
const site = loadSite()
const analyticsEnabled = site.facts.analytics.enabled
---

<Base title="Privacy" alwaysOffer>
  <article class="privacy">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <p class="privacy-lead">
      What this website does with a visitor's browser, and what it does not. It describes the
      site's own pages: the Fugaro program's posture is the <a href="/security/">security page</a>'s
      subject, and this page does not restate it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site collects</h2>

    {analyticsEnabled ? (
      <>
        <p>
          This site counts visits with <strong>Google Analytics</strong>, through Firebase
          Analytics. When you accept, the site loads Google's script and that script reports the
          page you are on to Google. It sets <strong>identifiers</strong> in your browser — a
          client id, kept in cookies or similar storage — which is how Google tells one visit
          from another.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site's own code asks for one page view per page and configures one setting,
          <code>anonymize_ip</code>. Nothing else about a visit is set here: no advertising, no
          remarketing, no profile of what you read elsewhere. What the Google script does beyond
          that, and what Google does with what it receives, is Google's own policy — this page
          does not restate it.
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>
        <strong>This site collects nothing.</strong> It is a set of static pages built from the
        files in the Fugaro repository: no account, no form, no comment box, and no code of the
        site's own running on a server. Nothing counts your visit, and nothing about it is sent
        anywhere. Should measurement ever be switched on, this page changes at the same time,
        and the site asks you first.
      </p>
    )}

    {analyticsEnabled ? (
      <>
        <h2>Analytics, and when it loads</h2>
        <p>
          Google's script loads only after you accept, and never before. Until then the page you
          are reading has made no request to Google at all: the script is not in the page, and no
          code fetches it. The banner at the foot of the window offers the choice, and the two
          answers are one press each:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Accept analytics</strong> loads Google's script, once, on this page.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Decline analytics</strong> loads nothing — and nothing on any later page
            either, until you accept.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Dismissing the banner without choosing — with Escape, or by reading on — counts as a
          refusal. The script needs a stored <em>yes</em> before it fetches anything, so a
          visitor who never answers is measured by nothing.
        </p>

        <h2>Giving and withdrawing consent</h2>
        <p>
          Your answer is kept in this browser only, and sent nowhere, under the key
          <code>fugaro:analytics-consent</code> in <code>localStorage</code>. The page's own
          loader reads it; nothing else does.
        </p>
        <p>
          To change your mind, use the banner on this page: it always offers the choice again,
          whichever answer you gave. Declining here withdraws consent — from your next page view
          the site loads nothing from Google. The page you are on keeps whatever it already
          loaded, because a page cannot un-request a script; closing or reloading it is enough.
          To remove the stored answer altogether, clear this site's data in your browser, and the
          next page you open asks again.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your browser refuses to store the answer — a private window, or site data turned off
          — the site loads nothing and asks again on your next visit. An answer that cannot be
          kept is not treated as one.
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        <h2>Analytics</h2>
        <p>
          This site has no analytics at all: nothing counts your visit, and no analytics script,
          cookie or identifier is involved in the page you are reading. There is therefore no
          consent to give here today, and nothing to withdraw.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        {analyticsEnabled
          ? 'It sets no cookie of its own, and — before you accept anything — it loads nothing that could set one.'
          : 'It sets no cookie of its own, and it loads nothing that could set one.'}
      </li>
      <li>It asks you for nothing: no account, no email address, no form to fill in.</li>
      <li>
        {analyticsEnabled
          ? "It loads nothing from another origin except Google's analytics script, and only after you accept. The typefaces are served from this site, and the build refuses a page that names another host."
          : 'It loads nothing from another origin — no font, no stylesheet, no script, no image, no embedded player. The typefaces are served from this site, and the build refuses a page that names another host.'}
      </li>
      <li>
        {analyticsEnabled
          ? 'It sells nothing, because it collects nothing to sell. The only thing that leaves your browser is what the analytics script sends, and only if you accepted it.'
          : 'It sells nothing, because it collects nothing to sell: nothing leaves your browser at all.'}
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What this page does not cover</h2>
    <p>
      This page describes the site's own pages. It does not describe the host's own request
      logs, which this repository does not control, or what the Fugaro program does on your
      machine — that is the <a href="/security/">security page</a>'s subject.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Scoped, so nothing here can reach another page, and token-only: the palette is W1.2's and
     this file carries no colour literal (website/test/a11y.mjs refuses one outside :root). */
  .privacy {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-xl) var(--space-md) var(--space-3xl);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .privacy h1 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-3xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }

  .privacy h2 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    margin: var(--space-2xl) 0 var(--space-sm);
  }

  .privacy p,
  .privacy li {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .privacy li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .privacy-lead {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-xl);
  }

  .privacy ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
  }

  .privacy code {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0 var(--space-3xs);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
